# a-crew-that-finds-the-work — plan 29 O2, and it is the THIRD of the
# triple `a-base-nobody-builds` / `a-base-the-player-builds` began.
#
# ⚠⚠ **It is `a-base-the-player-builds.keys` with the two crew lines
# moved TWO HEXES OFF the line they are meant to raise.**  Same ground,
# same core, same spawn, same wave list, same drive, same ticks spent —
# the crew are simply not standing on the work.
#
# ⚠⚠ **Before plan 29 O1 that base fell at 130** — the `nobody-builds`
# clock — because a crew member two hexes from a wall order stood there
# for the whole run (`plans/29` § O0 reading 1).  Every building
# scenario in the corpus parks the crew ON the work, and
# `a-base-the-player-builds.keys` says so in a comment: *"The crew,
# sitting on the line the player is about to draw."*
#
# ⚠⚠ **THE POINT: a PLAYER CANNOT DO THAT.**  There is no key that
# moves a crew member, so hand-placement is a fixture's privilege and
# never a player's.  What this scenario measures is the clock a real
# player gets — the semi-automatic default doing the placing.
#
# ── What the player does ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Parks at (5, -2), presses paint, and drives to (5, 2) — which orders
# FIVE hexes: the one it was standing on when the mode came on, plus the
# four it enters.  That is the band's whole walkable height, so the wall
# SEALS; `plans/12` § B7 measured that one with a gate in it buys
# nothing at all.  Two helpers standing two hexes off walk over and
# raise them — `numbers.json` § wall.build_time_wall is 10 s a hex for
# one helper, so five hexes is 50 helper-seconds and the pair need 25 s
# plus the walk.
#
# ⚠⚠ **The recon window is what pays for it** (`@X022`): the wave list
# does not start until the player pokes a spawn marker, so the ticks
# spent building are free — which is the design claim this pair makes
# measurable for the first time.

echo the crew find the work: a wall drawn by driving, raised by nobody's order

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

# ⚠⚠ The crew, standing TWO HEXES OFF the line the player is about to
# draw — inside an untrained crew member's own senses (3 hexes,
# `skill.loft::DETECT_BASE_HEXES`) and nowhere near the work.
crew 3 -1
crew 3 1

# ── The build (or not) ──────────────────────────────────────────
park 5 -2
paint on
drive 5 2
tick 6
count orders 5 5
paint off

# Park clear of the line so the trail is not rubbed out, and let the
# crew work.  60 helper-seconds over two helpers is 30 s = 45 ticks,
# plus the walk out to it — which nobody ordered.
drive 0 0
tick 50
count orders 0 0
count painted 80 80
# ⚠⚠ The whole band, hex by hex — because a wall with ONE gap in it
# buys nothing at all (`plans/12` § B7 measured a gate at +1 tick), so
# *five* is the number that matters rather than *some*.
kind 5 -2 wall
kind 5 -1 wall
kind 5 0 wall
kind 5 1 wall
kind 5 2 wall


# ── The waves — identical to `an-undefended-base` ───────────────
wave 5
count alive 5 5

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 13 13

# ── The clock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠⚠ **THE SEMI-AUTOMATIC DEFAULT IS WORTH THE WHOLE OF THE HAND
# PLACEMENT, TO THE TICK** (`@M069`) — the third reading of `@M050`'s
# pair, and the three are one token apart each time:
#
#   a-base-nobody-builds          130   no wall at all
#   a-base-the-player-builds      174   crew parked ON the line
#   a-crew-that-finds-the-work    174   crew two hexes off, nobody told
#                                       them anything
#
# ⚠⚠ **And the middle one is a fixture's privilege, not a player's**:
# there is no key that moves a crew member, so before plan 29 O1 the
# +44 ticks `@M050` measured could not be reached by anybody actually
# playing.  What this reading says is that the default hands them back.
#
# ⚠ It costs the walk and the walk is cheap — two hexes at 2.5 hex/s is
# under a tick, and the crew have 45 ticks of pre-wave window to use it
# in (`@X022`).  A base whose orders were further out than three hexes
# would read differently, and that is the point of the radius.
fall 900
ticks 172 176
count idle 0 2
snap o2_a_crew_that_finds_the_work
